{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's mechanism assumes 'Synthetic Diversity' gives a centralized system an edge, but it stipulates that the global control plane provides 'global visibility' to detect and isolate failing versions 'instantly.' This is a stronger redundancy assumption than the problem grants. The problem asks whether a large service with 'strictly more total internal redundancy' is more resilient to correlated failure; SRC_001 smuggles in a further requirement—central coordinated control with instantaneous global visibility—and then relies on that capability to win. A counterexample is a large service with strictly more redundant compute/storage but whose control plane is partitioned or itself subject to the same shared cause (e.g., the Leap Second bug): synthetic diversity cannot be 'braked' without the control plane, and the target's own countercondition (SPOF) is left as an unhandled observation rather than a refuted case, so the claim is not established under the problem's stated premises.","counterexample":[{"control_plane":"partitioned by the same shared cause that triggers version failure","outcome":"centralized system cannot isolate failing version; correlated failure propagates; decentralized network not obviously worse","synthetic_diversity":"present but uncoordinated once control plane is degraded","total_redundancy":"strictly greater than the decentralized network"}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"The target answers the problem on the wrong axis. The problem requires identifying what evidence would distinguish failures that are truly independent from failures that merely appear uncorrelated until a shared cause is found. SRC_002's mechanism ('Diversity of Incompetence' / anti-synchronization via noise) only argues that messy configurations dampen synchronization and prevent total collapse; it provides no evidentiary test for independence vs. latent correlation. Indeed, the 'noise' that makes failures look staggered could itself mask a common cause (e.g., a slow poison dependency) until it is discovered, so the target's premise risks conflating apparent lack of synchronization with genuine independence—the exact confusion the problem asks to resolve.","counterexample":{"actual_shared_cause":"all depend on the same upstream library with a slow memory leak; staggering is an artifact of differing load, not independence","distinguishing_evidence_required_by_problem":"none provided by SRC_002","inferred":"failures are independent","observed":"decentralized services fail at different times and in different ways due to noisy configs"},"target_alias":"SRC_002"}]}